title: "Running Plates and Persons"
description: "How dispatch handles record queries from road units and returns results over the air."
icon: "magnifying-glass"
---

Road units request record checks through dispatch. Dispatch runs the query, interprets the return, and relays only what the unit needs to act on.

## What the unit provides

<Tabs>

<Tab title="Plate query">

The unit gives the plate, phonetically, plus the province or state of issue.

Dispatch confirms by reading it back before running it. A wrong character produces a wrong owner, and acting on a wrong return is a serious error.

</Tab>

<Tab title="Person query">

The unit gives surname, given name, and date of birth. Full name and date of birth together are the minimum for a reliable match.

Names are spelled phonetically. Common surnames produce multiple matches, so dispatch must confirm the date of birth before returning anything.

</Tab>

</Tabs>

## Returning results

<Steps>

<Step title="Lead with anything that affects officer safety">
Weapons, violence history, or an outstanding warrant goes out first, before registration or address details. The unit needs to know what it is walking into.
</Step>

<Step title="State the status plainly">
Say whether the plate is valid, expired, stolen, or not on file. Do not leave the unit to infer it.
</Step>

<Step title="Give the detail second">
Owner, vehicle description, and expiry follow the status.
</Step>

<Step title="Note a mismatch">
If the vehicle described by the unit does not match the registration, say so explicitly.
</Step>

</Steps>

<Warning>
A hit is not a confirmation. Advise the unit that a result is unconfirmed until it has been verified, and say so on the air rather than leaving it implied.
</Warning>

## Channel discipline

Personal information stays off open channels wherever possible. Where a return contains sensitive detail, move the unit to a secondary channel or direct it to call by phone.

